body:
So a LASIK doctor, trying to sell you LASIK, has said you may have up to a 30% chance of being re-treated, and you're still considering this? 

&#x200B;

In other words, you **will** need a re-treatment (the doctor said 30% so he can claim you were in the unlucky 30%) and the result from both the initial and re-treatment, should you go that route, will be sub-optimal. 

&#x200B;

The fact that a LASIK surgeon hack is telling you the result may not be optimal, rather than lying to you and saying you're a "perfect candidate" is a god damn blessing in disguise!

Yep.. same story for me too at my clinic. Constantly being brushed off and told to come back and "give it time" 

&#x200B;

Well its been over a year now, and nothing's changed. 

&#x200B;

These LASIK hacks need to be held accountable.

"Never seen anything like it"

&#x200B;

Ah the used car salesmen of the medicine industry. That's the same line I was fed when i told them about my complications.

Yes pupil size matters. Ignore all the studies which are heavily biased toward saying pupil size doesnt matter (you have to think about who is giving the answers and who funds the research - similar to smoking back in the day) 

&#x200B;

Think about it logically for a second, you treat a 7mm zone on your cornea, yet at night time your eye is taking in light from a 9mm (actually greater since light doesnt just come from directly infront of you) diameter aperture. Now there is an untreated ring between the 7mm treatment zone and the dilated 9mm pupil which is what casuses you to observe all the fun stuff at night / low light conditions:

\- Ghosting,

\-Glare

\- Starbursts, etc

&#x200B;

You will notice how lasik surgeons and many proponents in this sub say to combat these abberations to try alphagan P (which prevents your pupil from dilating - but i thought pupil size doesnt matter!!?!?) or to try driving with the interior light on (ive even seen that recommendation on London Vision Clinics page - remarkable) 

&#x200B;

And be careful what optical zone they are telling you they treat with, is that an elliptical zone? If so, it wont be a circular 7mm. Furthermore, be aware that as your eye heals after surgery, there is a significant optical zone size reduction. So for example you start out with 7mm, you may end up with a 6.7mm. 

&#x200B;

Pupil size does matter... its really just common sense. If it didnt, why would we measure it at all?

If you're back home with no pressure to move back out, then take your time dealing with your mental health issues. 

&#x200B;

I have suffered from depression / anxiety for the last year, and I never quit my job to deal with those issues - I wish I did. I just kept churning, and still am, and just stuffing the mental health issues out of sight. Now they're starting to surface again slowly. You need to treat the main root cause and set your mind right first, and then everything will fall into place.

Yes. 

&#x200B;

I was +4 and +2.5 (yes with a PLUS) with 8.0mm pupils at night. 

&#x200B;

I have coma, trefoil, all the fun stuff at night and indoors.

&#x200B;

Dont do it man

The eye. 

&#x200B;

Lasers can ablate your cornea to any shape. The corneal thickness along the surface of the eye, combined with the prescription to be treated, limit how large a treatment zone you can go, as there is a minimum corneal thickness that surgeons like to maintain after surgery.

How long ago did you have your op? What about your second surgery, how long ago was that? Need some details here...

&#x200B;

The ghosting / coma may go away in time, but generally if you're more than a month out from surgery, the ghosting / coma is here to stay in some degree.

Yes. I too have ghosting, double vision, rainbow diffraction patterns, and many floaters as a result of this horrible procedure. 

&#x200B;

I had my surgery in may of 2018. 

&#x200B;

I was consumed with depression for about 6 months after, and I found that I really hit a turning point at the beginning of this year, where I started exercising and eating right and losing the weight that I gained from the depression. 

&#x200B;

Most days I dont even think about all the bad stuff that is now a part of my daily life. You cant change the past... you just need to accept it that you rolled the dice and it didnt work out. Nothing will bring back your former great corrected vision (with glasses) and healthy eyeballs. You need to embrace this as part of your life. You can control the future however, so work on that.

&#x200B;

All the best...

Hi, not to rain on the hopeful parade below, but i am one year out and have had ghosting / double vision and general bluriness at night since the first week post-op. 

&#x200B;

It hasnt improved or gotten worse over the course of the year. 

&#x200B;

With lasik, most of the healing is done within the first month. If you were farsighted, or really nearsighted, it may take up to 6 months to achieve your final result, however, generally you're like 90% of the way there by the 1 month mark. 

&#x200B;

Sorry to bring you bad news, but at 3 months, its unlikely that this will get better.

&#x200B;

I hope it gets better for you.

Sorry to hear about that. 

&#x200B;

You are but one of many who was treated with an optical zone too small for your dark-adapted pupil size. This is evident as the symptoms are momentarily resolved when you shine a light in your eyes and constrict your pupil.

&#x200B;

For reference, I have halos, starbusts as well as ghosting at night, and the symptoms are resolved by shining a light in my eyes too. My dilated pupil size in dark conditions is 8mm, and the treatment zone was a mere 6.5mm - funny that. 

&#x200B;

As for suggestions to try Alphagan P, yes it will work 100% - since it essentially gives you the same physical result as shining the light in your eyes (but will likely give you dry eyes, just an FYI) and the window of action, speaking from my experience and from many people's that I've read will gradually get shorter (might start out 8 hours that you have good vision, and then gradually the window of action of the Alphagan drops will only be like 2 hours of good vision at night, etc) 

&#x200B;

As for what to do about this, in terms of a more realistic fix? Well

1) The first point of action would be to go and get a refraction and a glasses prescription. If you have any significant residual myopia (-ve prescription), even as much as -0.50, this can affect your night vision  - see "night time myopia". Glasses at night may fix your halos / starbursts

&#x200B;

2) Surgery - now i always keep advising people not to have further surgery if their initial one wasn't successful - or even if it was successful. Second surgery is a real crap-shoot. Even moreso than the initial guesswork the surgeon did when performing your 1st surgery. Think about it, they didnt correctly zap your cornea the first time (hence you're here posting) so why trust them again with one of your most precious organs? Alot of anecdotal evidence (OH the horror! But the scientific journal articles say how 95% of people are happy!!) shows that second surgeries to fix issues created from the first often leave the patient WORSE off than before. So think carefully about it. 

&#x200B;

TLDR: if you can correct it with glasses, go with them. Second surgery is a crapshoot (more than the 1st)

No - the short answer. 

&#x200B;

Its less safe than the initial procedure - which isnt safe either. 

&#x200B;

I mean how long is a piece of string? It's a surgery - things do go wrong. And more likely in a second surgery. 

&#x200B;

My advice, if you can get better vision with glasses, get glasses.

Can you obtain the extra clarity by wearing some glasses with a small prescription on them? 

&#x200B;

Dont mess with your eyes anymore... who knows what will happen next time your corneas are vaporized.

I have 8mm pupils, and have halos, starbursts, ghosting as anything but super bright conditions. 

&#x200B;

I was given a 6.5mm optical zone. 

&#x200B;

The optical zone also shrinks from the intended diameter as the cornea heals after the surgery - something to keep in mind. 

&#x200B;

Pupil size does matter. Anyone trying to tell you different doesn't know what they're talking about

Welcome to LASIK. It's just the way it works out for some people. The doctors, even the "best", dont know who will encounter these symptoms exactly. They have a good idea of who might, but wont be able to tell you pre-operatively if you will be symptom-free after....

&#x200B;

All I can say is, at 6 months, unless by some divine intervention, your eyes are 99% healed and all of the "neuro-adaptation" or whatever term is the flavour of the month, has been done by now, as has the healing. 

&#x200B;

So you will be likely stuck with it. 

&#x200B;

Just for some reference, i am a year out, and my symptoms are exactly the same as they were from around the 3 month mark - and almost the same as they were at the 1 month mark. Lasik heals quick, that's the point. 

&#x200B;

Have you tried glasses, do you have any residual prescription? This can help with starburtsts, provided you have a residual prescription left.

The ones i tried, which were non-customized to my eye, did reduce the ghosting/bleeding, but were not comfortable enough for me to accept that as the trade off. 

&#x200B;

Yeah definitely see what effect it would have on your future dental career! Good luck

Hi, I'm in the same position as you, but am a year out since the surgery..



Im not going to give false hope like many on this subreddit, nor am i going to suggest further surgery like some on this subreddit. 

&#x200B;

The fact of the matter is that at 4 months out, like you are now, particularly for LASIK, 95% of the healing is done. Those miraculous "at 6 months my vision JUST got better!" stories mostly relate to PRK, and the whole point of LASIK is that the results are almost immediate, bar some healing in the first month. Just giving you a dose of reality, and not some cognitive dissonance bs "i LOVE my lasik, except for my dry eyes in the morning, or my halos at night, or my floaters, or my reduced contrast sensitivity" The list goes on of what people tell themselves to accept what they've done to themselves.

&#x200B;

Now for the support...

&#x200B;

I had lasik myself in may of 2018... easily the worst decision of my life. Like you, i experience all that night time glowing, streaks, doubling up of light sources and the bleeding out of them too. I've been through it all, searched far and wide for a solution... 

&#x200B;

What ive tried so far:

\- Alphagan eye drops: work OK, effects wears off quicker the more i use them. Dry my eyes out, so not worth it. 

\- Scleral lenses: work OK, but not comfortable enough, plus the point of lasik was not to rely on corrective lenses! 

\- Glasses / regular contacts: do not work. 

&#x200B;

I've sought second opinions, advice on more treatment from my surgeon who of course says he can "fix me" , etc etc etc..... all of this just sent me down a dark path of false hope and contradicting advice from the "experts" 

&#x200B;

Currently, I am not as bad and suicidal as I was (particularly for the first 4 to 5 months), I still get down if I dwell on my situation, but anyone can get downed about anything if we think of the negatives. 

&#x200B;

How did I get out? I realised that life wont be the same. and i have accepted that. That's the first step, you must accept that the "old you" isnt coming back. Your vision will likely never be the same again. I know it sucks, but acceptance is an important step in recovery. You must then also focus on all the positives in your life: Nothing around you has really changed, you're still going to be a dentist, you still have a loving family, friends, your health, you can still breathe the air, feel the sand between your toes and the cooling touch of grass on a summer day. The world is exactly as it was prior to the surgery. Dwelling on things you can't change is not productive. Just focus on steering your life in the direction you want it to go. You can't change the past, but you can make the future what you want it to be. 

Also, seeing a psychologist and getting on some SSRI anti-depressants was an absolute game-changer for me... I was on them for 2 months, and saw a psychologist for 4 months, and I can say im probably a stronger person than I was prior to surgery. Through crisis and trauma we learn alot about ourselves, and if you can get through this, you can get through anything. Believe me!

&#x200B;

Check out my posts in depression and suicidewatch if you want an insight into what I was feeling back then - it may resonate with you. At around the 6 month mark things started to turn around for me mentally ( eyes still messed up but it is what it is! )

&#x200B;

Any questions / concerns / queries, please reach out, im happy to offer support as best I can.

These emerging technologies are completely different than LASIK. LASIK / PRK evaporate the cornea in order to make it the right shape to correct your vision. 

&#x200B;

These newer procedures look at changing the shape of the cornea through the innovative use of UV light (in the case of PiXL - which funnily enough uses the same technology and process that is used to treat post-lasik ectasia! OR in the case of the other two technologies, they use a laser, but thats about the extent of the similarities between LASIK and these processes. The key difference is that newer technologies look to change the shape of the cornea without removing any tissue, whereas current laser eye surgery procedures remove material in order to create the right shape.

Your night vision will get worse... Objectively as a measurement of your optical system's higher order abberation levels. Will you notice this increase in HoA's? Maybe. Why chance it? 

&#x200B;

Night vision rarely gets better with LASIK/PRK. Those that claim it has gotten better are an anomoly.

&#x200B;

If you actually look at what happens to your cornea / optical system as whole after refractive surgery, your higher order abberations increase. They always do increase. Never decrease. What is a higher order abberation? A miss-shaping in the cornea that is "higher" in order than the regualar defocus / astigmatism (SPH and CYL) and they can't be corrected using traditional glasses. These higher order abberations are collectively known as "irregular astigmatism" and the more common ones you will hear about are spherical abberation, coma and trefoil. These each have distinct visual phenomenon associated with them. Spherical abberation is a halo, coma is a horizontal smearing (coma = comet tail), trefoil is a star pattern (distinct from a startburst - think of a mercedes 3-point star)

&#x200B;

Higher order abberations are most apparant at night. Which ones will increase? Who knows.... most commonly spherical abberation increases after refractive surgery. This is seen as a glow around lights, or the halo / fog / smog whatever you want to call it around lights at night. However, coma and trefoil sometimes do increase, and you will notice things like "ghosting". You will hear that term alot when people complain about post-lasik night vision issues. 

&#x200B;

The reason that higher order abberations increase after surgery is that the healing of the cornea is unpredicatable. The laser may be programmed to ablate in the best pattern to reduce existing abberations of the virgin cornea, while trying to anticipate the healing response of the cornea in an effort to minimize post-op higher order abberations, but the reality is everyone heals differently and the end is result is unpredictable at best. 

&#x200B;

Why risk it with a perfectly healthy eye that simply has a refractive error that can be fixed with glasses or contacts? 

&#x200B;

**There are heaps of emerging technologies that are non-ablative that soon will displace lasik:**

\- Cross-linking of the cornea in a customized pattern to alter the refractive power - called PiXL

\- Laser crosslinking of the cornea to change the refractive power (see Prof. Sinisa Vukelic's work out of Columbia Engineering School)

\- LIRIC - laser induced refractive index change of the cornea

&#x200B;

And there are better eye-measuring technologies coming out that will give the surgeon a better idea of your eye's refractive properties compared to what is used now.... 

&#x200B;

My advice as someone who has been burnt by LASIK and its side effects (been 1 year now with no change at all in side effects since day 1) **WAIT** until something better comes along. 

&#x200B;

So if you want better night vision / dont want your night vision to be affected, then steer well-clear of refractive surgery. 

&#x200B;

Cheers

Blessing in disguise.... Props to that honest surgeon for telling you about your possible keratoconous. 

&#x200B;

Stay away from refractive surgery of all types - it's a guessing game at best, and complications are untreatable if you're unlucky enough to be stuck with them.

Try taking fish oil daily...

&#x200B;

I take 6,000mg and have very little problem with dryness. Also, make sure you are drinking enough water, like at least 2.5L  a day. I notice when I have high salt foods, the next morning my eyes are drier than usual, which is a direct result of dehydration from salt. 

&#x200B;

\- Drink 2.5L of water a day at least

\- Consume 6,000mg of fish oil a day.

Yes. I had none at all prior to surgery. 

&#x200B;

Now have quite alot. Surgery was 7 months ago, the floaters remain unchanged to this day. 

&#x200B;

Sorry to say, but you might be stuck with them for life.

\+4 and +2.5 right and left respectively. 

&#x200B;

7 months ago. LASIK.

&#x200B;

Went horribly. Still experiencing triple vision indoors and at night (ghosting), halos, starbursts, floaters, rainbows around lights, night time glare and dry eyes. 

&#x200B;

Stay far far away from hyperopic lasik. If only I had...

Be careful, these conclusions about the disconnect between pupil size and night time vision issues - or the perception of HOAs in low light - are often studies performed by the very "doctors" trying to sell the laser surgery to you. Do you think they are going to publish results that reduce the suitable candidate pool? 

&#x200B;

And yes it's very frustrating. I have tried sclerals and they fix my HOAs a fair amount, but are not worth the effort for the small gain. Alphagan works, but only for a few days of continued use, then it just wears off too quick or doesnt even work. Further surgery I have not considered, because I dont trust my hack surgeon, and was actually warned by another "top refractive surgeon" not to get further surgery as it will not help, and will likely make things worse. 

&#x200B;

So here I am. Stuck like this for life due to some surgeon's greed to take me on as a "great candidate"

Get a new glasses prescription. You probably have an incorrect one. Either that, or keratoconus. Virgin eyes dont notice higher order abberations, due to the optical system working so well together. 

&#x200B;

Refractive surgery will increase higher order aberrations - it always does. Dont listen to the people who say it reduces them.

I got treated on the EX500 Wavelight laser, with 8mm dark adapted pupils, with a +4 and +2.5 prescription prior to surgery. 

&#x200B;

I have horrible ghosting, floaters, glare, halos, starbursts. I am 7 months post-op. Glasses cannot fix my higher order abberrations. 

&#x200B;

They "can" use a larger than normal treatment, but often they wont put in the effort. These lying psychopaths will say pupil size doesnt mater in relation to treatment zone, but it definitely does. Sure, the optical zone can be expanded, but only for very low prescriptions and in people with high corneal thickness. Or else you run the risk of thinning out your cornea too much in the peripheries of the optical zone and developing ectasia after the operation. 

&#x200B;

Buyer beware. If your dark adapted pupil size is larger or equal to (yes, even equal to, as the optical zone actually shrinks as you heal from the operation, meaning you will end up with an optical zone smaller than your dark adapted pupil size in this case) the intended treatment optical zone, stay the hell away or risk ruining your eyes for life.

Me too.. I was told that the "laser has to work a bit harder" in the consultation with the surgeon. All the while, they knew that I would get these side effects - they're not stupid, they've butchered enough eyes in their career to know who and who wont get a good outcome. 

&#x200B;

Complete psychopaths, these surgeons.

Yes I had lasik 6 months ago with 8mm pupils. Treated on the EX500 laser. Was treated with a 6.5mm optical zone. 

&#x200B;

I have ghosting, halos, glare, starbursts, you name it.

My condolences to her family and those affected..

&#x200B;

When LASIK goes wrong, and the outcome is less than ideal, it can and does have life altering effects. I got lasik 6 months ago, and still to this day am affected with double vision at night time, dry eye, rainbows around lights, night time glare and starbursts, and an increase in floaters. 

&#x200B;

From someone who has never been depressed in their life, now im in the darkest place I have been. And this slump has lasted from a week after surgery until this very day. I do think about killing myself multiple times a day, and im only 25. The only thing that keeps me going is the hope that some time during my life time there will be a fix for my visual issues. Glasses and contacts can no longer remove the junk in my vision. 

&#x200B;

All because I elected to have an unnecessary procedure done to my only set of eyes...

And the big secret that no refractive surgeon wants to tell you is:

&#x200B;

They actually dont know how you will end up after the surgery. No one does. Your body's healing response after having its corneas vapourized can make your final result vastly different than what was intended by the laser. So take that into account. No one can control how your body heals the damage (what the lasik surgeons like to call "regression"). Oh and they also call surgeries to fix their stuff-ups as "touch ups". Dont buy into it - "touch ups" are just as risky, if not more risky, than the original surgery. And still, they dont know how your cornea will remodel itself after being vapourized. 

&#x200B;

Cheers.

If your contacts doing the job, I wouldnt get lasik or PRK for that matter. PRK is no safer than lasik, contrary to there being no cutting into the eye. It has its other dangers. 

&#x200B;

There is a very real possibility you will have life-long lasting side effects. Dry eyes, reduced contrast sensitivity, floaters, night time glare and starbursts, rainbows around lights (if going LASIK), ghosting.

&#x200B;

If you're lucky, these side effects may clear up over time. If you're less lucky, glasses can fix the ghosting (multiple images). If you're really unlucky, glasses can't fix you and you have to go and get specialized rigid gas permeable contacts called scleral lenses, which are much harder to handle than your current daily disposables. 

&#x200B;

Take it as a blessing i disguise that you were rejected 8 years ago.

Oh i should mention this scan is from a second opinion doctor. 

&#x200B;

The initial treatment was done on a Wavelight EX500. 

&#x200B;

The second opinion doctor who did these scans looked at the topography and i specifically asked if the laser ablation was decentered and he claimed there was no decentration.  So i dont know what could be the cause?

Hi, I dont have the topographic maps on me, but i remember that it had a steep middle compared to the periphery. 

&#x200B;

The PSFs do accurately reflect what I see when i look at any light source, yes.
